# Cloudflare Pages headers. The CSP is the product claim made browser-enforced:
# with connect-src 'none' this page is structurally incapable of transmitting,
# and anyone can verify that by reading the response headers instead of
# trusting a privacy policy.
#
# default-src 'none' matters as much as connect-src: a remote image load with
# data in the query string is a perfectly good exfiltration channel, and most
# CSPs leave img-src wide open.
#
# font-src 'self' exists because the house fonts (Inter, JetBrains Mono) are
# self-hosted; the sibling sites load them from Google, which this CSP forbids.
#
# connect-src 'none' is also why build.sh inlines the wasm as base64: the stock
# wasm-pack loader fetches its module, and fetch is blocked here. The header is
# not negotiable, so the loader changed instead.
#
# Permissions-Policy: microphone is OFF. The decode-rate audio feedback is
# output-only (AudioContext), which needs no permission. The Phase 2
# data-over-sound reverse channel will need microphone=(self) restored.
#
# Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy is deliberately absent: it was here for
# SharedArrayBuffer, which this build does not use, and require-corp only adds
# ways for the page to fail. Phase 2's decode worker will need it back.
# Cloudflare applies EVERY matching rule and *appends* repeated headers rather
# than replacing them. A response carrying two Content-Security-Policy headers
# is governed by both, and a request must satisfy every policy — so a broad
# `/*` policy cannot be relaxed for one path by a later rule, it can only ever
# be narrowed. The `/sw.js` block below used to be written as if it replaced
# this one; the live origin returned both, the worker inherited
# `connect-src 'none'` on top of its own `'self'`, and every precache fetch was
# refused. The app had no offline support in production at any point, silently,
# while a local server that merged the rules reported it working.
#
# So the CSP is not set here. It is set per document, on the only two responses
# that can carry a policy: the page and the worker. A CSP on a .js, .css or
# .woff2 response governs nothing — only documents and workers get policies —
# so nothing is lost by leaving those without one. Everything else in this
# block is a header that genuinely should apply to every response.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), bluetooth=(), serial=(), midi=(), interest-cohort=()

# The service worker must never be served stale, or deployed updates are
# invisible until the HTTP cache expires.
#
# It also needs its own CSP, and this is not a detail. A CSP served with a
# worker script governs that worker's global scope, and `cache.addAll` fetches
# through it like anything else. Under the `/*` policy above the worker inherits
# `connect-src 'none'`, so every precache fetch is refused, install rejects, and
# the app is left with no offline capability at all — silently, because a failed
# install has no symptom until someone turns the network off and finds out. That
# is the product's headline claim failing closed with no error anywhere.
#
# So the worker gets `connect-src 'self'` and nothing else opens up. This does
# not touch the page's policy: the page still cannot fetch, cannot connect, and
# cannot transmit, which is the claim a sceptical user verifies with curl. And
# the worker cannot be turned into a courier for the page, because it fetches
# only its own origin (enforced twice: here, and by the origin check in its
# fetch handler), and it answers exactly two postMessage types, neither of which
# fetches anything.
#
# The page's own policy, on both URLs that serve it. `./` and `./index.html`
# are both precached, so both have to carry it: a navigation served from the
# cache replays the headers the response was stored with.
#
# This is the product claim, and it is still verifiable exactly as before:
#   curl -sI https://rabaska.favreau.xyz/ | grep -i content-security-policy
/
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' blob: data:; media-src 'self' blob:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'none'; manifest-src 'self'; form-action 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/index.html
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' blob: data:; media-src 'self' blob:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'none'; manifest-src 'self'; form-action 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# This block does NOT replace the policy above, it is added to it — which is
# why the page CSP no longer matches this path at all. See the note on /* .
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'
